WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.715
Mission intake captures the request before resources are committed.

00:00:04.715 --> 00:00:15.266
It should identify the requester, purpose, decision need, location, timeframe, required data, urgency, privacy impact, deliverable, funding or authority, and known constraints.

00:00:15.266 --> 00:00:23.124
An approval workflow tests whether the mission is lawful, necessary, feasible, proportionate, and within organizational capability.

00:00:23.124 --> 00:00:30.981
Safety, aviation, site, privacy, legal, security, and data owners may review different parts according to risk.

00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:37.941
Scope defines what the crew will fly, observe, and deliver, along with explicit exclusions.

00:00:37.941 --> 00:00:42.655
A bounded scope prevents mission creep and unnecessary collection.

00:00:42.655 --> 00:00:49.615
Changes should return through a documented approval path rather than being improvised during flight.

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:52.084
Roles must be clear.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:55.901
The pilot in command holds flight-safety authority.

00:00:55.901 --> 00:00:59.717
Visual observers support airspace and hazard awareness.

00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:02.187
Payload operators manage collection.

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:04.656
Mission leads coordinate objectives.

00:01:04.656 --> 00:01:07.126
Data reviewers validate products.

00:01:07.126 --> 00:01:09.595
Maintainers control airworthiness records.

00:01:09.595 --> 00:01:18.351
Escalation paths cover rule ambiguity, weather, equipment defects, privacy concerns, emergency events, data exposure, and pressure to continue.

00:01:18.351 --> 00:01:25.759
Staff need permission to pause or reject a mission without being overruled by scheduling pressure.

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:31.821
The operating model should define handoffs and records from intake through closure.

00:01:31.821 --> 00:01:42.822
Governance succeeds when every important decision has an owner, evidence, and review path, while the pilot retains the authority to stop unsafe flight.

00:01:42.822 --> 00:01:50.230
Set realistic service levels for intake, ordinary review, urgent escalation, scheduling, data delivery, and correction.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:58.537
Clear expectations prevent requesters from bypassing governance because they assume the approved process will be too slow.
